oq-2 evidence (fix round 1): the cmd/verdi "infeasible" claim, re-probed
with Go's per-package test timeout raised.

WHY. The first version of the README reported cmd/verdi as NOT ANSWERED /
infeasible on the strength of a 600.587s FAIL inside gremlins' own
coverage-gathering pass (whole-module-dryrun.log:137-149). 600s is Go's
DEFAULT per-package test timeout, and gremlins passes no -timeout flag on
that pass at all — the instrumented trace in
oq1-gremlins-mainpkg-defect.txt shows the coverage argv verbatim:

  ARGV=test -cover -coverprofile /var/folders/.../coverage ./cmd/e2eharness/...

No -timeout. `gremlins unleash --help` exposes --timeout-coefficient, but
that only scales the timeout of the PER-MUTANT runs
(internal/engine/executor.go:227, `args = append(args, "-timeout",
(2*time.Second + m.testExecutionTime).String())`); there is no knob for
the coverage pass. So the question the spec needs answered is whether
cmd/verdi's suite is "slow today under load" or "out of reach", and that
is answerable without gremlins: run the same pass with the timeout raised.

WHAT WAS RUN. In a throwaway local clone of the scratch worktree detached
at e963f4d0 (/tmp/fix-mr), nothing else started by this session running
concurrently:

  $ uptime
  22:23  up 16 days, 15:10, 6 users, load averages: 29.53 27.76 23.52
  $ go test -timeout 30m -cover -coverprofile /tmp/fix-mr-cmdverdi.cov ./cmd/verdi/...
  ok  	github.com/jyang234/verdi/cmd/verdi	431.731s	coverage: 61.3% of statements
  real 439.59
  user 170.51
  sys 172.55
  exit status: 0
  $ uptime
  22:30  up 16 days, 15:18, 6 users, load averages: 3.96 11.46 17.03

Load fell from 29.53 to 3.96 across the run (other lanes' work finishing,
not this session's).

WHAT IT MEANS.

1. cmd/verdi's suite is NOT out of reach: it passes in 431.731s, 7.2
   minutes, with coverage. The earlier 600.587s FAIL was the default
   10-minute wall being blown under heavier load, not a suite that cannot
   finish.

2. But the margin is thin and the wall is unconfigurable. 431.731s against
   a hard 600s default is 1.39x of headroom, in a pass gremlins gives no
   flag to extend. The earlier FAIL is therefore reproducible whenever the
   machine is ~40% busier than it was here — which it was, for most of this
   spike's session.

3. It is not the binding blocker anyway. cmd/verdi is `package main`, so
   gremlins v0.6.0 cannot measure it at all: every mutant would be scored
   KILLED by a `[setup failed]` that never compiles the code
   (oq1-gremlins-mainpkg-defect.txt). Raising the coverage timeout would
   buy a 431.731s baseline pass followed by a report that is 100% false.

4. If the main-package defect were fixed, the per-mutant cost would be set
   by this same figure: gremlins' per-mutant timeout is
   2s + elapsed*coefficient, i.e. 2s + 431.731s*10 = ~72 minutes per
   mutant at the settings this spike used, and each mutant run pays up to
   one full suite. That is the number co-3's budget has to survive, and it
   is why cmd/verdi-sized packages need a different strategy (a -run-scoped
   subset, or exclusion) rather than a bigger timeout.

STATUS: the probe was RUN, once, not declared untried. cmd/verdi's oq-2
row stays NOT ANSWERED for mutant counts, but the reason is corrected: the
tool cannot measure `package main`, and the timeout is a secondary,
quantified risk rather than the cause.
